  "query_preview": "..\nTuesday through Thursday: Broad ridge pattern will keep dry, \nwarm conditions over the region for the next several days. Winds\nwill be light, diurnal driven with strongest winds along the \neast side of the Cascades. Wind gusts will be in the teens for \nthe area. For temperatures, highs will climb into the 90s with\nhumidity in the teens. There is a 50-70% probability for \nportions of the Lower Columbia Basin, including Wenatchee and \nLewiston, on reaching 100 on Thursday. Wildfire smoke will play \na big part on the highs. It could possible shave off a few \ndegrees. Overnight lows will be in the 50s to low 60s. \n\nFriday and Saturday: A weak trough will press into the region.\nIt will bring a zonal flow pattern. Winds will increase as warm\ndry conditions continue. Winds will be West with sustained\nspeeds in the teens and gusts into the 30s. One major \ndifference from August 1 is the lack of strong low level winds.\n850-700mb winds are only reaching into the 15-25 mph range \ncompared to the 30-40 mph from August 1. It is still an \nelevated to critical fire weather period due to the continued \nhot and dry conditions with the breezy winds. Highs will be in \nthe upper 80s and 90s with humidity in the teens to low 20s. \nOvernight lows will be in the 50s to low 60s. \n\nSunday through Monday: Cooler air mass will filter into the\nregion. There is still no decent chance of of precip expected on\nfor the period. Highs will dip into the 80s. Lows will be in the\nupper 40s and 50s. /JDC",
